==English==
[[File:Magnificent CME Erupts on the Sun - August 31.jpg|right|thumb|a '''filament''' of solar material]]
[[File:Carbonfilament.jpg|right|thumb|a glowing light bulb '''filament''']]

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|ML.|fīlāmentum}}, from {{der|en|LL.|fīlō||to spin, draw out in a long line}}, from {{der|en|la|fīlum||thread}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɪləmənt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-filament.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[fine]] [[thread]] or [[wire]].
# Such a wire, as can be heated until it [[glow]]s, in an [[incandescent]] [[light bulb]] or a [[thermionic valve]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1979|month=August|author=Graham Burtenshaw; Michael S. Welch|title=O.V.S. Bulleid's SR loco-hauled coaches - 1|journal=Railway World|page=398|text=Lighting was unimaginative for the standard stock with naked tungsten '''filament''' bulbs and metal reflectors. However, all compartments had individual reading lights above the seats with attractive glass shades.}}
# {{anchor|en-n-astro}} {{lb|en|physics|astronomy}} A [[massive]], [[thread]]-like [[structure]], such as those [[gaseous]] ones which extend outward from the surface of the [[sun]], or such as those (much larger) ones which form the boundaries between large [[void]]s in the universe.
#: {{ux|en|solar '''filament'''}}
#: {{ux|en|galaxy '''filament'''}}
#: {{ux|en|the Ursa Major '''Filament'''}}
# {{lb|en|botany}} The stalk of a [[flower]] [[stamen]], supporting the [[anther]].
# {{lb|en|textiles}} A [[continuous]] [[object]], limited in [[length]] only by its [[spool]], and not cut to length.

